  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Jordan Agriculture Engineers Association (JAEA) has institutionalized on the job training approaches to certify the skills of agriculture workers; 243 workers received training in different agricultural occupations, 69 of whom women, 114 Syrians, 82 Jordanians, and 47 migrants from different nationalities. The ILO coached JAEA representatives on the on-the-job training methodology and helped equip their training team to conduct OJT in remote areas.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.3.1. Number of member States that have applied ILO approaches to work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Jordanian government national skills centres- the National Institute of Training (NIT) and the Vocational Training Centre (VTC)- implemented inclusive skills programmes and services for persons in vulnerable situations, including vulnerable Jordanians, women, and Syrian refugees, by adopting ILO skills development methodologies, including On-the-job-training and employment (OJT&amp;E). As a result, in 2020, 428 people (264 men and 164 women) received on-the-job-training (OJT), where 70% were Syrians, 7%, Jordanians and the remaining 23% from other nationalities. A total of 717 beneficiaries (287 men and 430 women) received inclusive vocational training and employability skills development programmes, out of which 290 were Jordanians and 427 Syrians. In 2021, the ILO provided skills development to 453 beneficiaries, including 179 female trainees and 266 Syrian trainees, related to several professions, including mosaic, electrical extensions technician, furniture painting, home painting, plumbing (solar heating), plastering, gypsum panel, sanitary piping, and furniture carpeting. With support from the governments of the United States and Germany, the ILO provided technical inputs for the development and launching of the skills certification programmes. The ILO also supported the training centres to identify market relevant occupations in consultation with the relevant industry representatives and monitored the quality of the training.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.3.2. Number of member States with innovative, flexible and inclusive skills programmes and services targeting women, youth or persons in vulnerable situations</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Technical and Vocational Skills Development Commission (TVSDC) along with the social partners institutionalized National Sector Skills Council (NSSC) by: (a) formulating the bylaws for NSSCs;  (b) creating a directorate to look  after sector skills council affairs; and (c) developing National Terms of Reference for NSSCs. 

The NSSCs for Garment and Leathers (G&amp;L) and Chemicals and Cosmetics (C&amp;C) conducted skills anticipation and skills match assessment to identify demands of the priority occupations through field surveys covering around 600 firms in each sector. These two NSSCs formulated Sector Skills Strategies and supported the development of National Occupational Standards for 13 occupations in the G&amp;L, C&amp;C and P&amp;R sectors to make the skills training industry demand responsive. With the support of the government of the Russian Federation, the ILO supported TVSDC and the social partners in setting up three Sector Skills Councils for Garment and Leathers (G&amp;L), Chemicals and Cosmetics (C&amp;C), and Plastic and Rubbers (P&amp;R) sectors comprising business leaders and representatives from the government and trade unions. The ILO under the PROSPECT project promoted another NSSC for the agriculture sector. It also supported TVSDC in formulating the bylaws for NSSCs and developed Terms of Reference for NSSCs in partnership with several international and national development partners.

The ILO also provided technical advice and capacity building to G&amp;L and C&amp;C sector skills councils to conduct a skills anticipation study, develop sector skills strategies and occupations standards through the &quot;Applying the G20 Strategy&quot; project.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.1.2. Number of member States with institutionalized national or sectoral mechanisms to measure skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.2. Increased capacity of member States to strengthen skills and lifelong learning policies, governance models and financing system</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Technical and Vocational Skills Development Council (TVSDC) digitalized and automated the TVET services of its three core directorates and redesigned all the work flow process for the TVET services provided by the TVSDC resulting in considerable improvement in efficiency and transparency of skills governance. As a result, students of all categories, including those from the most vulnerable groups, can get these services from anywhere in the country instead of travelling to Amman. TVSDC brought legislation for training provider licensing and accreditation standards for improved quality assurance of the skills delivery.
It designed a digital career guidance and employability focussed portal that brings together employers, job seekers and educators to support lifelong learning. Based on this design, a portal was set up.

Public schools delivered market oriented career guidance to help ease refugee and host community students&apos; transitions into the labor market using a manual developed by the ILO and endorsed by the Ministry of Education to support lifelong learning. As a result 3,715 youth (16-24) - aapproximately 35% Syrian and 46% female- received professional development and career counselling through one-on-one and small group sessions, focused on technical and vocational training pathways to employment. Around 170 parents participated in the Online Community Events in collaboration with MOE. With the support of the government of the Russian Federation, the ILO provided technical support for undertaking process engineering of all the work flow processes for TVET services and automate these services through digitization.   

The ILO also provided technical support in: (a) drafting the legislation for trainer provider licencing; (b) designing digital career guidance service.

In addition, the ILO supported the development of the career guidance manual for career counsellors and students entitled: &quot;My Future Career Path Manual&quot; (in Arabic and English) and trained 47 counsellors from Ministry of Education schools and inside Zaatari refugee camp schools.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.2.2. Number of member States with inclusive skills and lifelong learning governance models</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Technical and Vocational Skills Development Council (TVSDC) strengthened its Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) system and included Syrian female and male refugees for testing their skills for certification in construction, confectionary and garment sectors. Altogether 6,700 Jordanian and Syrian refugee workers (45% women) have been tested and given occupational licences. The skills certification helped the Syrian refugees to get work permit that facilitated their transition to the labour market. 

The TVSDC adopted a practical exam at the work place to test and certify the skills of agriculture workers. The ILO supported TVSDC for piloting RPL certification system relying on the observation of skills at the work place along with a written test to certify workers with lower levels of literacy, and advocated for TVSDC to introduce a certification system.  

At the TVSDC request, the ILO is supporting TVSDC to strengthen the RPL system further by introducing a systemic approach and building the institutional capacity further. The ILO intervention is also directed towards improving the quality of testing and expanding the coverage of occupations in diverse sectors for skills recognition.</narrative>
   </description>
